        ":question/id": "f284d9d4-47c8-54c3-aa2b-11e7ba22d40f",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-79697",
        ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{{x}^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$"
      },
